{ Again, white cannot play the main move. This time the black king is on the edge of the board. In this case, checking from the diagonal adjacent to the rook is best. }
1. Qd5+ Kf8 2. Kg6 Re6+ 3. Kf5 Re1 { Black's best move is to adopt a distant defense. } 4. Qc4 { White's key idea is to control the checking square, f1. Note that the black king cannot leave the 1st rank without conceding a fork. } 4... Rb1 5. Qd3 { White queen controls both f1 and a6. } 5... Rb7 6. Qd6+ { Giving check from the diagonal next to the diagonal of the rook } 6... Kg7 7. Qd4+ Kh7 8. Kf6 Rg7 9. Qh4+ Kg8 10. Qh5 { Philidor position } *


