[UTCTime "05:20:38"]

1. Qf7+ { Queen gives check and covers both the h5 and e6 checking squares } 1... Kc6 { Black king moves toward center } 2. Qe8+ { Again, queen gives check and covers both checking squares, h5 and e6 } 2... Kc7 3. Kd5 { Now white can bring the king forward, because there is only one check since the queen covers the c6 square. } 3... Rd6+ 4. Kc5 { Cage position } 4... Rd7 { Best defense for black is to drop back and block the queen's diagonal. An alternative is retreat to a6, the side away from the queen. } (4... Ra6 5. Qe7+ Kb8 { In this position, the queen controls both d8 and e8, so black cannot sustain a 3rd rank defense. The queen controls d6,e6,and f6 directly, and g6 and h6 indirectly through forks, so attacking the rook by moving the king to b6 covers a6, b6, and c6 and forces the rook to move off the 3rd rank. } 6. Kb5 Ra7 7. Qe5+ Kb7 8. Qd4 Kb8 9. Kb6 Rb7+ 10. Ka6 { Diagonal cage } 10... Kc8 11. Qh8+ Kc7 12. Qg7+ { skewer }) 5. Qe5+ { White gives check with the queen... } 5... Kb7 { Giving black the choices of b7 or d8. } (5... Kd8 6. Qb8+ Ke7 7. Kc6 Rd8 8. Qc7+ Ke8 { Absolute seventh } 9. Qg7 Rd1 10. Qg4 { attack the rook } 10... Rd8 11. Qg5 { Trapezoid position } 11... Rd7 12. Qg8+ Ke7 13. Qg7+ { skewer } 13... Ke6 14. Qxd7+) 6. Kb5 { If b7, then white follows with his king. } 6... Rc7 7. Qd5+ { After rook rejoins king, white simply repeats the pattern. } 7... Kc8 { Wishbone on back rank, invert } (7... Ka7 8. Qe4 Rf7 9. Qd4+ Kb8 10. Qd6+ Ka7 11. Kc6 Rb7 12. Qd8 { Philidor position }) 8. Qa8+ Kd7 9. Kb6 Rc8 10. Qb7+ Kd8 { Absolute seventh } 11. Qf7 Rc1 12. Qf4 { attack the rook } 12... Rd1 13. Qh4+ Kc8 14. Qg4+ { fork } 14... Kd8 15. Qxd1+ *


