1. Qc6+ Kd8 2. Qd5+ Ke7 3. Qc4 { Here we examine 3 Qc4 from the previous position, which looks plausible. Now the black king has access to both central files, which is why Qd3 is superior. } 3... Kd8 { Now the black king can run away to the queen-side if we keep checking on the a6-f1 diagonal. We can deal with this by switching neighboring diagonals. } 4. Qd4+ { Now e8 is the only square for the black king that doesn't result in an immediate fork. } 4... Ke8 5. Qa4+ { And now the white queen switches to the other diagonal. } 5... Kf7 6. Qc4+ { Controlling the checking square f1 } 6... Ke8 7. Kf6 { Bring the king closer, threatening mate on c8. } 7... Kd7 8. Qd3+ Ke8 9. Qb5+ Kd8 10. Qa5+ Kd7 11. Qxe1 *


