[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/methurst"]
[FEN "8/4k3/2Q5/6r1/3K4/8/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.07.13"]
[UTCTime "12:25:18"]

{ A common starting position for a harassment defense is when black maintains a cut-off defense on his fourth rank. White has just played Qc6, taking one central square away from the black king. }
1... Rh5 2. Ke4 { If black played Rg5, then we would reach the killing position for the third rank defense, being applied here on the fourth rank. } 2... Rh1 { Instead, black plays Rh1, threatening a series of checks either from rank 1 or the h file. Because the rook is sheltered from forks by the white king, the black king is relatively free to blockade any progress by the white king. } 3. Qc5+ Ke6 4. Qe5+ Kd7 5. Qg3 { With Qg3, white covers both of the checking squares e1 and h4. } 5... Ke6 6. Qg4+ Kd6 { This is the first key position to remember in defeating a harassment defense. Queen and black king stand at the corners of a 4x3 rectangle, the queen shelters the king from checks on h4 and black's only remaining check from e1 is met by Kd4, with the queen covering d1. White's best plan is to approach the black king. } 7. Kf5 Kc5 { Black's best defense is to move his king. White responds by setting up a discovered check. The queen shelters the king from check on rank 5 and a check from f1 would be met by the discovered check, ending the harassment. } 8. Qg5 Ra1 { This is black's most prolonged defense, but perhaps not his most effective. } 9. Ke4+ { White's king walks toward the rook, where the final assault must occur. } 9... Kb4 10. Qd2+ Kb3 11. Qd3+ Kb2 12. Qd4+ Kb1 13. Kd3 Ra3+ 14. Kd2 Rb3 { This diamond position will be reviewed extensively later. The key move for white is Qe5, maintaining control of the long diagonal. } 15. Qe5 Rb6 16. Qe4+ Ka2 17. Qc4+ Kb1 18. Kc3 Rb2 19. Qa4 { Philidor position } *


