{ This same position can occur with black's king on the 1st rank. In this case, the rook will go to h2. This happens when black elects to use a harassment rather than 3rd rank defense. }
1... Rh2 2. Qc8+ { This is now slightly quicker than Qc6+, while before they were roughly equivalent. } 2... Ke7 3. Qg4 { The queen covers both of the checking squares e1 and h5, and creates the same 4x3 block relative to the black king. } 3... Rb2 { Because the white king began on the 5th - rather than 4th - rank, we are closer to mate than before. But white must know the specific forcing variation here, or black can escape. } 4. Qd4 Rh2 5. Kf5 Rh5+ 6. Kg4 Rb5 { The white pieces directly control squares c5-h5, and the queen can fork a5 and every square on the h file. b5 is the black rook's only move. } 7. Kf4 Rh5 8. Qg7+ Kd8 9. Qf8+ Kd7 10. Qf7+ { fork } 10... Kc6 11. Qxh5 { Successful outcomes for black include:
1) Giving a series of harassing checks which waste moves, or
2) Threatening to give a series of checks while white moves about trying to prevent them.
Successful outcomes for white include:
1) If black starts giving checks, white can end the checks while also making progress.
2) Preferably, black shifts to a close or distant defense.
3) Forcing one of the two key zugzwang positions. } *


